From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christophe Pettus <xof@thebuild.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 23:01:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 33/35] H23: charge JBIG2 symbol instances to a work allowance
 from max_image_pixels

A text region places up to 10,000 symbol instances, and so does every
aggregate symbol of a dictionary. The memory budget bounds the symbols a
decode allocates but not how often it draws them, so a few kilobytes could
draw a large symbol 10,000 times per region, region after region: finite,
but with no bound on CPU, and out of page_time_budget's reach since that is
checked only between operators.

Each placed instance is now charged its symbol's width x height (unclipped)
plus 64 for decoding the placement against a per-decode work allowance,
DecoderContext::work_budget, carried in the MemoryBudget handle that
already reaches every place that draws an instance. hayro-syntax sets it to
JBIG2_WORK_PIXELS_PER_PIXEL (16) x max_image_pixels, as many pixels as the
memory budget can allocate as one-bit bitmaps, so a decode may draw each
pixel it could hold once. A text page draws each glyph about once, so this
leaves a fourfold margin at the cap and far more for ordinary scans; at the
default cap the costliest shape (one-pixel-wide symbols, about 0.45 ns per
charged pixel) then stops within a few seconds. Exceeding it fails with
OverflowError::WorkBudget, which hayro-syntax maps like the memory budget
to DecodeFailure::ImageMemoryLimit, so the image is skipped with an
image: memory limit warning. A default context has no allowance.

Fixes internal #116
